BECOME A TECHNICAL OFFICIAL

Help Deliver Fair Competition

The Obstacle Course Racing Association of Ireland (OCRA ÉIREANN) is developing a national pathway for Technical Officials within obstacle sport.

Technical Officials, commonly known as TOs, help ensure that competitive OCR is delivered fairly, consistently and in accordance with the applicable competition rules.


What Does a Technical Official Do?

Technical Officials may be responsible for areas including:

- Monitoring obstacle completion
- Applying competition rules
- Recording penalties
- Monitoring course boundaries
- Supporting starts and finishes
- Recording incidents
- Supporting results verification
- Assisting with protests and reviews
- Supervising competition areas
- Supporting other Technical Officials

More experienced TOs may progress into roles involving greater responsibility for competition delivery and Technical Official teams.


Who Can Become a TO?

You do not need to be an elite OCR athlete.

Technical Officiating may suit:

- OCR athletes
- Former athletes
- Coaches
- Club members
- Volunteers
- Race organisers
- Officials from other sports
- People interested in competition rules and event delivery

Good Technical Officials need:

- Attention to detail
- Fairness
- Confidence
- Good communication
- The ability to remain impartial
- A willingness to learn


Learn the Rules

Technical Officials need a strong understanding of the competition rules applying to their role.

Education may cover:

- Obstacle completion
- Penalties
- Competition procedures
- Athlete conduct
- Course observation
- Incident reporting
- Protest procedures
- Technical Official responsibilities


Gain Practical Experience

Technical Officiating is a practical role.

New officials should have opportunities to work alongside experienced TOs at suitable events and build experience across different obstacles, disciplines and competition formats.


Progress as a Technical Official

As experience develops, officials may progress into more senior responsibilities including:

- Leading obstacle teams
- Supervising competition areas
- Briefing Technical Officials
- Supporting National Championships
- Reviewing incidents
- Supporting protest processes
- Managing officiating teams
- Working towards international-level events


Why Become a TO?

Technical Officiating offers a way to contribute directly to competitive obstacle sport.

It can provide opportunities to:

- Support Irish OCR events
- Work at National Championships
- Learn the sport in greater technical detail
- Support athletes and organisers
- Develop officiating experience
- Contribute to fair competition
- Progress towards higher-level events


